# philosophy.domain-as-a-garden

domain.operations are decomposed, reusable, idempotent, low-trust contract components. they encapsulate true domain knowledge and make it safe and easy to recompose into more powerful mechanisms.

## .gardeners and architects

> "architects do blueprints before they drive the first nail. gardeners dig a hole, plant the seed, and see what comes up." — george r.r. martin

both are required: architect each solution. but architects who think like gardeners build more evolvable systems — tomorrow's blueprint should build on what grew, not tear it down. **decomposition for recomposition matters more in the long term than the elegance of any single design.**

## .symbiogenesis

> "life evolves spontaneously in environments capable of computation … and grows more complex over time as it enters symbiotic relationships with itself." — blaise agüera y arcas

margulis: complex cells arose when unlike bacteria merged (mitochondria were once free bacteria). so too operations: atomic operations compose into behaviors neither could reach alone; idempotent contracts keep identity stable.

## .recomposable units

| property | enables |
|---|---|
| idempotent | safe retry, safe recomposition |
| low-trust contracts | explicit inputs/outputs, no hidden inputs |
| single responsibility | clear boundaries for composition |
| domain-aligned | speaks ubiquitous language |

`syncCustomerFromStripe` did not exist when `getOneCustomer`, `setCustomerPhone`, `genInvoice` were written; when it was needed, the pieces were ready. dynamically-stable: free to evolve and recompose, stable because each piece is safe to invoke again.

> decomposition for recomposition is the core mechanism of dynamically-stable domain.operations.

applies to domain.objects too, through narrower bounds. full text, sources, and quotes: `philosophy.domain-as-a-garden.[philosophy].md`.
